// Test Description: While loop with many transient substring allocations and no outer-scope escape
// Category: control
// Dependencies: none
// Expected: PASS
//
// Regression for FRAME-UI-ASSEMBLE-PAGE-COMPANION-NO-ROUTES-MOUNTED
// (fp e8ce04051112). Codegen regression introduced by `8c25d971`
// (removed per-iteration mem_scope_push/pop from all loop forms).
//
// The removal was necessary to fix RUNTIME-CONSECUTIVE-IF-ITERATE-DROPPED
// (per-iter pop freed memory that escaped via outer accumulator
// assignment), but it overcorrected: loops whose body allocates many
// short-lived strings and DOES NOT assign them to outer-scope variables
// now leak every allocation until the host's per-request scope_pop. For
// long-running plugin entry points (e.g. frame.ui's `find_unescaped_quote`
// scanning a large JSON body with thousands of substring calls) this
// exhausts plugin memory and produces a `string.substring` trap halfway
// through `assemble()`.
//
// This test exercises the exact safe shape: a `while` loop whose body
// allocates a fresh substring each iteration but never assigns it to an
// outer-scope reference. The fix detects "no outer ref escape" + "no
// early return" at MIR build time and restores per-iteration scope
// push/pop for that case. RUNTIME-CONSECUTIVE-IF-ITERATE-DROPPED stays
// fixed because its body has `errs = add_err(errs, ...)` — an outer ref
// assignment that disqualifies the loop from per-iter scope.
//
// 4000 iterations × ~10 bytes per substring ≈ 40 KB of transient
// allocations. Without per-iter reclaim, the standalone wasmtime runner
// (1 GB heap) tolerates this, so the FUNCTIONAL guard here just verifies
// the loop terminates with the right count — the bit-level guard is in
// `tests/test_loop_iter_scope.rs` (asserts the emitted WASM contains
// `mem_scope_push`/`mem_scope_pop` calls inside the loop body).
//
// Expected output:
//   matched=4000

start:
	integer i = 0
	integer matched = 0
	string source = "abcdefghij"
	while i < 4000
		string ch = source.substring(i % 10, (i % 10) + 1)
		if ch == "a" or ch == "b" or ch == "c" or ch == "d" or ch == "e" or ch == "f" or ch == "g" or ch == "h" or ch == "i" or ch == "j"
			matched = matched + 1
		i = i + 1
	printl("matched=" + matched.toString())
